<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00656</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a70a7c05-aaa5-4cfc-9d3c-3e4bac428d8a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">For 1-2, use this situation:</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
        <quote id="quote-00001">Jada has <emphasis>p</emphasis> pennies and pennies and <emphasis>n</emphasis>
        nickels that add up to more than 40 cents. She has fewer than 20 coins altogether. nickels
        that add up to more than 40 cents. She has fewer than 20 coins altogether.</quote>
     </para>


        <exercise id="_2f51990c-2c29-4de5-8a0a-67ead22d1fd8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Which two inequalities could make up a system of inequalities that represents how
              many pennies and nickels that Jada could have? Select <emphasis>two</emphasis>
              inequalities.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.01</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.05</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>0.40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the total number of coins. The problem states that she
              has fewer than 20 coins. The number of pennies plus the number of nickels added
              together will be less than 20 coins.</para>
            <para id="para-00007"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.01</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.05</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>0.40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the value of each coin. The value of a penny is
              1 cent (0.01) the value of a nickel is 5 cents (0.05). The number of pennies
              multiplied by its value of 0.01 plus the number of nickels multiplied by its value
              0.05 will give the total amount of money she has. The problem states that she has more
              than 40 cents.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Try again. Revisit activity 2.15.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.01</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.05</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>0.40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, \({p+n&lt;20, 0.01p +
              0.05n &gt;0.40}\).</para>
            <para id="para-00010"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the total number of coins. The problem states that she
              has fewer than 20 coins. The number of pennies plus the number of nickels added
              together will be less than 20 coins.</para>
            <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.01</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.05</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>0.40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the value of each coin. The value of a penny is
              1 cent (0.01) the value of a nickel is 5 cents (0.05). The number of pennies
              multiplied by its value of 0.01 plus the number of nickels multiplied by its value
              0.05 will give the total amount of money she has. The problem states that she has more
              than 40 cents.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_925506b4-4947-41a4-bbe4-bbea49b9c31a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">For questions 2 – 4:</para>
            <para id="para-00013">
              <quote id="quote-00002">Determine if it is possible that Jada has each of the following combinations of
              coins. If so, explain or show how you know. If not, state which constraint—the amount
              of money or the number of coins—it does not meet.</quote>
           </para>
            <para id="para-00014">15 pennies and 5 nickels</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00015">Correct! She has fewer than 20 coins. 15+5=20, it is not less than 20</para>
            <para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit activity 2.15.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is No. She has fewer than 20 coins. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, is not less
              than 20.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_21ecf5c7-cd2d-4ebb-885b-2fcfc2691222" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00018">16 pennies and 2 nickels</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct! She has more than 40 cents. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>26</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, is not more than 40 cents.</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Try again. Revisit activity 2.15.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is No. She has more than 40 cents. 16+2(5)=26, it is not more
              than 40 cents.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2ad63535-86d2-44d5-bc24-852604216899" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00022">10 pennies and 8 nickels</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct! She has less than 20 coins. 10 +8=18. That is less than 20 coins. She has
              more than 40 cents. 10+8(5)=50, which is more than 40 cents.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Revisit activity 2.15.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is Yes. She has less than 20 coins. 10+8=18. That is less than
              20 coins. She has more than 40 cents. 10+8(5)=50, which is more than 40 cents.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6fa0f6f8-4c66-472a-a6cf-25ec091dfce5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00026">A triathlon athlete swims at an average rate of 2.4 miles per hour, and bikes at
              an average rate of 16.1 miles per hour. At the end of one training session, she has
              swam and biked more than 20 miles in total. The inequality 2.4<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> + 16.1<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> &gt; 20
              and this graph represent the relationship between the hours of swimming, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the
              hours of biking, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and the total distance the athlete could have traveled in
              miles.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Mai said, "I'm not sure the graph is right. For example, the point (10,3) is in
              the shaded region, but it's not realistic for an athlete to swim for 10 hours and bike
              for 3 hours in a training session! I think triathlon athletes generally train for no
              more than 2 hours a day."</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Which inequality represents Mai's last statement?</para>
            <para id="para-00029">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00030"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00031"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00032"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00033"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00034">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00035">Try again. Revisit activities 2.15.3 and 2.15.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00036">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Mai states that hours swimming, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, plus hours
              on the bike, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, are less than or equal to (since they train no more than) 2. This
              is represented by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Mai states that hours swimming, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, plus hours on the
              bike, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, are less than or equal to (since they train no more than) 2. This is
              represented by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_38088c2a-93b8-4519-85d3-24ea75467a2d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00037">For questions 6 and 7, use the following situation and graph:</para>
            <para id="para-00038">
              <quote id="quote-00003">Elena is considering buying bracelets and necklaces as gifts for her friends.
              Bracelets, \(b\),  cost $3, and necklaces, \(n\), cost $5. She can spend no more than
              $30 on the gifts. Elena needs at least 7 gift items. This graph represents the
              inequality \(3b+5n≤30\), which describes the cost constraint in this situation.</quote>
           </para>
            <!--6-->
            <para id="para-00039">Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of bracelets and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> the number of necklaces.
              Which inequality represents the number of gift items that Elena needs?</para>
            <para id="para-00040">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00041"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00042"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mi>≥</m:mi><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00043"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00044"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00045">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mi>≥</m:mi><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00046">Try again. Revisit activities 2.15.3 and 2.15.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00047">The correct answer is b+n≥7.</para>
            <para id="para-00048">Elena needs at least 7 gifts that are a combination of number of bracelets, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>,
              and number of necklaces, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, so added together <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> must be greater or equal to
              7.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ebdd1dc8-1108-4b98-9590-5f76be2e725f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00049">Which of the following could be solutions to the system of inequalities? Select <emphasis>
              two</emphasis> answers.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00050">Correct! (7,1), (6,2)</para>
            <para id="para-00051">Try again. Revisit activities 2.15.3 and 2.15.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00052">The correct answers are (7,1), (6,2).</para>
            <para id="para-00053">(7, 1), (6, 2) are possible solutions since when substituted into the inequality,
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>≥</m:mi><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>≥</m:mi><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3412537d-7fbe-432a-9ba0-b1b08abc7518" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00054">A: The combination of 7.5 cubic feet of topsoil and 12 cubic feet of compost is
              within the gardener's budget.</para>
            <para id="para-00055">A gardener is buying some topsoil and compost to fill his garden. His budget is
              $70. Topsoil costs $1.89 per cubic foot, and compost costs $4.59 per cubic foot.
              Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> statements or representations that correctly
              describe the gardener's constraints in this situation. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the cubic
              feet of topsoil and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> the cubic feet of compost.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">B: If the line on the graph represents the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.89</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> + 4.59<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> = 70,
              this graph represents the solutions to the gardener's budget constraint.</para>

            <media alt="Graph of an inequality.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_17_10_01.svg"/>
            </media>

            <para id="para-00057">C: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.89</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>+4.59<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>≥70</para>
            <para id="para-00058">D: The combination of 5 cubic feet of topsoil and 20 cubic feet of compost is
              within the gardener's budget.</para>
            <para id="para-00059">E: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.89</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>+4.59<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>≤70</para>
            <para id="para-00060">F: If the line on the graph represents the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.89</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>+4.59<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>=70, this
              graph represents the solutions to the gardener's budget constraint.</para>
            <para id="para-00061">

              <media alt="Graph of an inequality.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_17_10_02.svg"/>
              </media>

           </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00062">Correct! The combination of 7.5 cubic feet of topsoil and 12 cubic feet of compost
              is within the gardener's budget., <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.89</m:mn><m:mi>\</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>+4.59<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>≤70\), If the line on the graph
              represents the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.89</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4.59</m:mn><m:mi>\</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>=70\), this graph represents the solutions to
              the gardener's budget constraint.</para>
            <para id="para-00063">Try again. Revisit activities 2.15.3 and 2.15.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00064">Correct!</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
